{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 225 10 "
Problem 3." }{TEXT 287 3 ".  " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 0 "" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 56 "There is a one-to-one correspondence bet
ween subsets of " }{TEXT 226 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 287 28 " \+
and binary lists of length " }{TEXT 227 1 "n" }{TEXT 287 17 ", that is
, lists " }{TEXT 228 3 "L =" }{TEXT 287 1 " " }{TEXT 229 22 "[x1, x2 ,
 . . . , xn] " }{TEXT 287 6 "where " }{TEXT 230 18 "x1, x2, . . . , xn
" }{TEXT 287 25 " are elements of the set " }{TEXT 231 5 "\{0,1\}" }
{TEXT 287 57 ".  The correspondence is given by associating to the set
 " }{TEXT 232 1 "S" }{TEXT 287 10 " the list " }{TEXT 233 1 "L" }{TEXT
 287 7 " where " }{TEXT 234 34 "xi = 1 if i is in S and 0 if not. " }
{TEXT 287 78 "For example, the set \{1,3,5\} corresponds to the list [
1,0,1,0,1,0,0] if n = 7." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 1 "\n" }{TEXT 
235 3 "(a)" }{TEXT 287 19 " Write a procedure " }{TEXT 236 12 "list_to
_set " }{TEXT 287 79 "whose input is a binary list and whose output is
 the corresponding set. E. g., " }{TEXT 237 24 "list_to_set([1,0,1,0,1
])" }{TEXT 287 21 " will return the set " }{TEXT 238 7 "\{1,3,5\}" }
{TEXT 287 44 ". Note that nops(L) is the length of a list." }}{PARA 0 
"" 0 "" {TEXT 287 1 "\n" }{TEXT 239 3 "(b)" }{TEXT 287 19 " Write a pr
ocedure " }{TEXT 240 11 "set_to_list" }{TEXT 287 22 " whose input is a
 pair" }{TEXT 241 4 " S,n" }{TEXT 287 7 " where " }{TEXT 242 1 "S" }
{TEXT 287 16 " is a subset of " }{TEXT 243 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }
{TEXT 287 5 " and " }{TEXT 244 1 "n" }{TEXT 287 69 " is a positive int
eger and whose output is the binary list of length " }{TEXT 245 1 "n" 
}{TEXT 287 26 " corresponding to the set " }{TEXT 246 1 "S" }{TEXT 
287 23 ". E. g., if n = 5 then " }{TEXT 247 22 "set_to_list(\{1,3,5\},
5)" }{TEXT 287 13 " will return " }{TEXT 248 11 "[1,0,1,0,1]" }{TEXT 
287 2 ".\n" }{TEXT 287 1 "\n" }{TEXT 249 3 "(c)" }{TEXT 287 59 " Show \+
by a few tests that each procedure works. Then apply " }{TEXT 250 11 "
set_to_list" }{TEXT 287 32 " to each set in the powerset of " }{TEXT 
251 12 "\{1, 2, 3, 4\}" }{TEXT 287 158 " to form all binary lists of l
ength 4. Make a program to print out a table of the following form. (B
ut the order need not be the same as that started below.)\n" }{TEXT 
287 1 "\n" }{TEXT 287 24 "   [0,0,0,0] <-->  \{  \}\n" }{TEXT 287 24 "
   [1,0,0 0] <--> \{ 1 \}\n" }{TEXT 287 24 "   [0,1,0,0] <--> \{ 2 \} 
" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 12 "    ........" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 287 7 "    etc" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 
"" {TEXT 287 108 "Some extra commas in the output is okay. You may obt
ain the power set of the set \{1,2,...,n\} by the command " }{TEXT 
252 12 "powerset(n);" }{TEXT 287 37 " but you must first load the pack
age " }{TEXT 253 9 "combinat." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
0 28 "list_to_set:=proc(n::list);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "a:=nops(n);\n" 
}{MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "S:=\{\};\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "for i to a do\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 40 "if n[i]=1 then S:= S union \{i\}; end if;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "return S;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
0 9 "end proc:" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 1 "\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "list_to_set([1,
0,1,0,1]);" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 288 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 288 "Warning
, `a` is implicitly declared local to procedure `list_to_set`" 4 "http
://www.maplesoft.com/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Warning,%20%60
a%60%20is%20implicitly%20declared%20local%20to%20procedure%20%60list_t
o_set%60" "" }{TEXT 288 0 "" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 288 0 "" }
{HYPERLNK 288 "Warning, `S` is implicitly declared local to procedure \+
`list_to_set`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/support/help/errors/view.as
px?path=Warning,%20%60S%60%20is%20implicitly%20declared%20local%20to%2
0procedure%20%60list_to_set%60" "" }{TEXT 288 0 "" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 ""
 {TEXT 288 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 288 "Warning, `i` is implicitly declared lo
cal to procedure `list_to_set`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/support/he
lp/errors/view.aspx?path=Warning,%20%60i%60%20is%20implicitly%20declar
ed%20local%20to%20procedure%20%60list_to_set%60" "" }{TEXT 288 0 "" }}
{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "<%\"\"\"\"\"$\"\"&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 ">
 " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "restart:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 29 "set_to_list:=pro
c(s::set,n);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "#a:=nops(s);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "#b
:=max(s);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "#L:=[];\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 22 "for i from \+
1 to n do \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "  if i in s then L[i]:=1;\n" }{MPLTEXT
 1 0 25 "  else L[i]:= 0; end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "end do;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 27 "return [seq(L[i],i=1..n)];\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "end pr
oc:" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 288 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 
288 "Warning, `i` is implicitly declared local to procedure `set_to_li
st`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Wa
rning,%20%60i%60%20is%20implicitly%20declared%20local%20to%20procedure
%20%60set_to_list%60" "" }{TEXT 288 0 "" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 
288 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 288 "Warning, `L` is implicitly declared local to \+
procedure `set_to_list`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/support/help/erro
rs/view.aspx?path=Warning,%20%60L%60%20is%20implicitly%20declared%20lo
cal%20to%20procedure%20%60set_to_list%60" "" }{TEXT 288 0 "" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "set_to_list(\{1,5,7\},10);\n
" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 4 "[r];" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 1 "\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "r:=[1,
2,3,4,5];\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "has([r],2);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "7,\"\"\"\"\"!F$F$F#F$F#F$F$F$" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "7#I\"rG6\"" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 ">I\"rG6\"7'\"
\"\"\"\"#\"\"$\"\"%\"\"&" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "I%trueG%*pro
tectedG" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 254 11 "Problem  4." }{TEXT 
287 1 " " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 287 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
287 13 "Note that if " }{TEXT 255 1 "i" }{TEXT 287 20 " is an integer \+
then " }{TEXT 256 18 "convert(i,base, 2)" }{TEXT 287 14 " will convert
 " }{TEXT 257 2 "i " }{TEXT 287 47 "to a binary list (the binary repre
sentation of " }{TEXT 258 1 "i" }{TEXT 287 37 " in reverse order). The
n you may use " }{TEXT 259 11 "list_to_set" }{TEXT 287 46 " to convert
  this list to a set. Note that if " }{TEXT 260 1 "i" }{TEXT 287 11 " \+
runs from " }{TEXT 261 1 "0" }{TEXT 287 4 " to " }{XPPEDIT 205 0 "Type
setting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \+
\"italic\"), Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-msup(Typesetting:-mn(\"2\"
, font_style_name = \"CharacterStyle256\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), \+
Typesetting:-mi(\"n\", italic = \"true\", font_style_name = \"Characte
rStyle256\", mathvariant = \"italic\"), superscriptshift = \"0\"), Typ
esetting:-mo(\"&minus;\", font_style_name = \"CharacterStyle256\", mat
hvariant = \"normal\", fence = \"false\", separator = \"false\", stret
chy = \"false\", symmetric = \"false\", largeop = \"false\", movableli
mits = \"false\", accent = \"false\", lspace = \"0.2222222em\", rspace
 = \"0.2222222em\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"1\", font_style_name = \"Charac
terStyle256\", mathvariant = \"normal\")), Typesetting:-mi(\"\", itali
c = \"true\", mathvariant = \"italic\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6
\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#miGF$6%Q!F'/%'italicGQ%trueF'/%,mathv
ariantGQ'italicF'-F#6%-I%msupGF$6%-I#mnGF$6%Q\"2F'/%0font_style_nameGQ
2CharacterStyle256F'/F3Q'normalF'-F,6&Q\"nF'F/F>F2/%1superscriptshiftG
Q\"0F'-I#moGF$6.Q(&minus;F'F>FA/%&fenceGQ&falseF'/%*separatorGFO/%)str
etchyGFO/%*symmetricGFO/%(largeopGFO/%.movablelimitsGFO/%'accentGFO/%'
lspaceGQ,0.2222222emF'/%'rspaceGFhn-F;6%Q\"1F'F>FAF+" }{TEXT 287 6 " t
hen " }{TEXT 262 32 "list_to_set(convert(i,base,2) ) " }{TEXT 287 33 "
 will run through all subsets of " }{TEXT 263 16 "\{1, 2, . . ., n\}" 
}{TEXT 287 3 ". \n" }{TEXT 287 1 "\n" }{TEXT 264 3 "(a)" }{TEXT 287 
42 " Use this idea to make your own procedure " }{TEXT 265 9 "PowerSet
 " }{TEXT 287 17 "which will given " }{TEXT 266 1 "n" }{TEXT 287 27 ",
  produce the powerset of " }{TEXT 267 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT
 287 87 ". Show by several examples that your procedure works. For you
r examples you should get " }{TEXT 268 24 "nops(PowerSet(n)) = 2^(n" }
{TEXT 287 32 "). Check that this is the case.\n" }{TEXT 287 2 " \n" }
{TEXT 269 3 "(b)" }{TEXT 287 35 " Use this idea to make a procedure " 
}{TEXT 270 7 "RandSet" }{TEXT 287 43 " which given n produces a random
 subset of " }{TEXT 271 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 287 7 ". [Use 
" }{TEXT 272 4 "rand" }{TEXT 287 42 " to produce a random integer in t
he range " }{TEXT 273 8 "0..2^n-1" }{TEXT 287 36 " and then convert it
 to a subset of " }{TEXT 274 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 287 1 "."
 }{TEXT 275 3 "]  " }{TEXT 276 31 "Do NOT use PowerSet or powerset" }
{TEXT 287 240 ". Show by examples that it works for small n such as 5,
 10, and 20 as well as for large n such as 100. It will even work for \+
n = 1000, but the output will be rather large. On average a random sub
set of \{1,2,...,n\} will contain n/2 elements." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 277 7 "[Remark" }{TEXT 287 62 ": The powerset of a set with 1000
 elements contains more than " }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-mrow(Types
etting:-mi(\"\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \"italic\"), Typeset
ting:-mrow(Typesetting:-msup(Typesetting:-mn(\"10\", executable = \"fa
lse\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"300\", executable \+
= \"false\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), superscriptshift = \"0\")), Ty
pesetting:-mi(\"\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \"italic\"));" "-
I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#miGF$6%Q!F'/%
'italicGQ%trueF'/%,mathvariantGQ'italicF'-F#6#-I%msupGF$6%-I#mnGF$6%Q#
10F'/%+executableGQ&falseF'/F3Q'normalF'-F;6%Q$300F'F>FA/%1superscript
shiftGQ\"0F'F+" }{TEXT 287 171 " elements. This is, according to curre
nt estimates, more than the number of atoms in the whole universe. So \+
you cannot expect your computer to be able to store such a set." }
{TEXT 278 1 "]" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}
